  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "4+Qy4ylqMYPvwj0CikBTqIAxLoQ=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:46.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "\"Where does this actually run?\" is the question that unlocks the rest of the stack, and for reducers the answer is unambiguous: inside the Reactor, and specifically inside whichever Reactor processes the action.\n\n## The three tempting wrong answers\n\n**\"Inside Connect, because that's where the user is.\"** Connect is a host that embeds a Reactor. The user's keystroke becomes an action, Connect dispatches it, and the reducer runs in the embedded engine \u2014 not in Connect's React tree.\n\n**\"Inside Switchboard, because it manages the API.\"** Switchboard hosts a server-side Reactor, but its GraphQL and MCP layer is a surface, not an execution site. A mutation resolver dispatches; the Reactor reduces.\n\n**\"On the DocSync layer, as part of conflict resolution.\"** DocSync moves operations between Reactors. Reducers run *before* that \u2014 they are what produce the operations DocSync then transports. Nothing on the wire re-runs a reducer as part of merging.\n\n## The mental model\n\nHosts dispatch, the Reactor reduces. Draw the boundary once and every subsequent question answers itself: an editor cannot \"call the reducer,\" because it has no reducer to call \u2014 it hands an action across the boundary and reads back derived state. A subgraph resolver is in the same position. Both are clients of the engine.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nBecause the reducer runs in exactly one place per action, its purity has teeth. The same action dispatched from a browser editor and from a server-side integration produces the same operation, because it is reduced by the same code in the same kind of engine. Validation you put in a reducer therefore holds no matter which surface fired the action \u2014 you cannot bypass it by going around the UI to the API. Conversely, business logic placed in an editor or a resolver holds only for that surface, which is why it belongs in the reducer instead.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/02-Learn/02-architecture/01-the-big-picture.mdx (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:35:46.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:46.000Z"
    }
  },
